1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
This is RelayDesk, an event-link handover tool.
Volunteers may change, but the event's identity should stay stable.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Delegate one editing permission—not a wallet,
domain ownership, or control over every record.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:32,000
There are three entry points: a wallet-free simulation,
real Sepolia operations, and test-name registration.

4
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Simulation changes only this browser's sample state.
The evidence page separately shows verified on-chain transactions.

5
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:57,000
At the core is ENSv2's Permissioned Resolver.
An organizer can delegate the url record for a specific name.

6
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:07,000
We use the actual permission system, not a mock contract.
An ordinary wallet directly owns the registered name.

7
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Registration uses free test stablecoins,
with an allowance limited to the required amount.

8
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:27,000
First, the organizer grants the volunteer url-editing permission.
The volunteer can then publish a new event link.

9
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:39,000
The name remains unchanged.
Next, the organizer revokes the volunteer's permission.

10
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:50,000
The same volunteer's next update is denied.
Revocation preserves the last successfully published link.

11
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:02,000
This is more than a simulation.
We registered relaydesk2026.eth on Sepolia.

12
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Two different OKX accounts completed the real grant,
edit, and revocation. The explorer links show those transactions.

13
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:25,000
After revocation, all four permission scopes are zero.
Further writes are denied, while the event link remains intact.

14
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Before signing, the app rechecks the account, network,
target contract, and transaction contents.

15
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Receipts and on-chain effects are verified; uncertain sends stay locked.
The app never reads private keys or approves wallet prompts automatically.

16
00:02:50,000 --> 00:03:02,000
Codex generated and verified the code and automated tests.
Claude cross-reviewed technical sources and assumptions.

17
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:15,000
I directed the goals and key choices, prepared accounts,
and participated in operation and verification. Next: real organizer feedback.
